title:string = "The AEON System"
description:string = "A map of the AEON declaration ecosystem: Core syntax, AES, AEOS validation, meaning validation, canonical representation, Tonics, profiles, and conventions."
activePage:string = "ecosystem"
keywords:list<string> = ["ecosystem", "system", "layers", "AES", "AEOS", "canonical", "tonics", "profiles", "conventions"]

page:node = <page(
  <section @{type:string = "feature", level:string = "1"} (
    <kicker("AEON System")>
    <title("AEON is a layered declaration ecosystem.")>
    <heading("A declaration is not a runtime object.")>
    <copy("Most data formats begin by treating data as an object, tree, map, or document. Useful systems then grow around that foundation: schemas, serializers, runtime mappers, conventions, transforms, and execution surfaces.")>
    <copy("Over time those layers can collapse into each other. Structure becomes mixed with meaning. Validation becomes mixed with execution. Metadata becomes mixed with content. Parsing becomes mixed with materialization.")>
    <copy("AEON starts from a different premise: a declaration is not the same thing as a runtime object. From that distinction, the rest of the system follows.")>
    <actions(
      <action @{href:string = "#language-layer", primary:boolean = true} ("Language layer")>
      <action @{href:string = "#aes"} ("AES")>
      <action @{href:string = "#contracts"} ("Contracts")>
      <action @{href:string = "/walkthrough-processing.php"} ("Processing walkthrough")>
    )>
  )>

  <section @{type:string = "feature"} (
    <kicker("Core Shift")>
    <title("AEON preserves meaning without prematurely executing it.")>
    <copy("AEON is not merely a serialization format. It is a layered declaration ecosystem built around deterministic [& assignment streams|AES].")>
    <copy("Rather than collapsing every concern into a single object model, AEON keeps identity, structure, metadata, relationships, validation, interpretation, and materialization as separate responsibilities.")>
    <code(`
source text
  → syntax
  → assignment stream
  → form validation
  → meaning validation
  → materialization
`)>
    <copy("Each phase moves forward. No layer quietly rewrites the meaning of an earlier layer.")>
  )>

  <section @{type:string = "feature", id:string = "language-layer"} (
    <kicker("Language Layer")>
    <title("Different constructs carry different responsibilities.")>
    <copy("Traditional formats often force identity, structure, metadata, and relationships into one structural model. AEON lets them coexist without collapsing into one another.")>
    <referenceList(
      <item(
        <name("Bindings")>
        <definition("map-centric identity and canonical addressing.")>
      )>
      <item(
        <name("Containers")>
        <definition("object, list, and tuple structure for nested values.")>
      )>
      <item(
        <name("Nodes")>
        <definition("ordered document-shaped structure for mixed content and markup-like trees.")>
      )>
      <item(
        <name("Attributes")>
        <definition("hard metadata attached locally to a binding, anonymous value, or node.")>
      )>
      <item(
        <name("References")>
        <definition("symbolic relationships through clone and pointer references.")>
      )>
      <item(
        <name("Comments")>
        <definition("soft metadata and annotation channels outside the ordinary value stream.")>
      )>
    )>
  )>

  <section @{type:string = "feature"} (
    <kicker("Bindings")>
    <title("Bindings create identity.")>
    <copy("A [& binding|Binding] is not just a surface convenience. It declares that a value is assigned to a canonical path.")>
    <code(`
user:object = {
  name:string = "Patrik"
}

paths:list<string> = [
  "$.user"
  "$.user.name"
]
`)>
    <copy("That stable identity gives tools something precise to target: diagnostics, schema rules, references, canonical comparison, hashing, and audit output.")>
  )>

  <section @{type:string = "feature"} (
    <kicker("Containers")>
    <title("Containers organize structure without pretending every shape is a domain object.")>
    <copy("Objects, lists, and tuples provide hierarchical structure. They group values, preserve order where order matters, and give authors ordinary document shapes without requiring runtime materialization.")>
    <code(`
config:object = {
  db:object = {
    host:string = "localhost"
  }
  ports:list<number> = [3000, 8080]
  point:tuple = (:number = 12, :number = 42)
}
`)>
    <copy("Structure and runtime meaning remain separate. A container can be valid AEON long before a consumer decides what it means inside an application.")>
  )>

  <section @{type:string = "feature"} (
    <kicker("Nodes")>
    <title("Nodes preserve ordered document structure.")>
    <copy("Nodes let AEON represent ordered, nested, document-like structures without forcing them into map/list shapes. They are useful for templates, prose-adjacent data, and markup-like content.")>
    <code(`
content:node =
  <paragraph (
    "Hello "
    <strong ("world")>
  )>

template:node =
  <article (
    <h1(~document.title)>
    <p(~document.summary)>
  )>
`)>
    <copy("[& AEON Core] preserves the node tree as declared. A [& Tonic] may later materialize it as HTML, Markdown, &ND, a UI component tree, or something domain-specific, but that materialization is an explicit consumer choice.")>
  )>

  <section @{type:string = "feature"} (
    <kicker("Metadata")>
    <title("Attributes and comments are different kinds of side channels.")>
    <copy("[& Attributes|Attribute] are hard metadata: inline, structured, and attached to the thing they qualify. [& Semantic comments|Semantic comment] are soft metadata: advisory, annotative, or workflow-oriented side channels that do not become ordinary data bindings.")>
    <code(`
title@{lang:string = "en"}:string = "Hello"

//# User-facing display title
title:string = "Hello"

//@ review.status("draft")
`)>
    <copy("This separation lets a document carry provenance, rendering hints, semantic labels, profile hooks, documentation, and governance metadata without flattening all of those roles into content.")>
  )>

  <section @{type:string = "feature"} (
    <kicker("References")>
    <title("References preserve relationships symbolically.")>
    <copy("Clone references express value intent. Pointer references express address or identity intent. Keeping that distinction visible lets tools preserve dependency relationships instead of collapsing every reference into a terminal value immediately.")>
    <code(`
db:object = {
  host:string = "localhost"
}

hostCopy:string = ~db.host
activeDb:object = ~>db
`)>
    <copy("Indirection itself can be meaningful. Runtime materialization can resolve it later under consumer policy.")>
  )>

  <section @{type:string = "feature", id:string = "aes"} (
    <kicker("AES")>
    <title("The Assignment Event Stream is the semantic spine.")>
    <copy("The center of the AEON ecosystem is not a runtime object. AEON Core produces an ordered stream of assignment events that tools can inspect before meaning is accepted.")>
    <code(`
AEON source
  → lexer
  → parser
  → canonical path resolution
  → AES emission
  → AEOS validation
  → meaning validation
  → Tonic materialization
`)>
    <copy("Each event can preserve canonical path, source order, spans, datatype hints, attributes, references, and original structure. That stream becomes the stable handoff point between tools.")>
  )>

  <section @{type:string = "feature"} (
    <kicker("AEOS")>
    <title("AEOS validates form without interpreting meaning.")>
    <copy("[& AEOS] answers structural questions: does this path exist, does the value have the expected literal family, does the representation satisfy the schema, and is the document shape acceptable?")>
    <copy("It does not coerce values, resolve references, execute processors, or materialize domain objects. That restraint is what makes validation usable as a boundary.")>
    <code(`
schemaCheck:validation = {
  path:string = "$.moon.hoursBeforeMidnight"
  required:boolean = true
  expectedType:string = "IntegerLiteral"
  result:toggle = on
}
`)>
  )>

  <section @{type:string = "feature"} (
    <kicker("Meaning Validation")>
    <title("Sound data still needs contextual understanding.")>
    <copy("After AEOS has accepted the representation, a [& consumer|Consumer] may still need to ask whether the [& claims|Claim] make sense in the domain. This layer brings in context, policy, conventions, and application knowledge without making AEOS guess at meaning.")>
    <code(`
patientBodyTemp@{unit:string = "cm"}:float = 99.8

meaningCheck:validation = {
  path:string = "$.patientBodyTemp"
  representation:toggle = on
  issue:string = "body temperature uses a length unit"
  expectedUnit:list<string> = ["celsius", "fahrenheit"]
  result:toggle = no
}
`)>
    <copy("The value can be syntactically valid and structurally acceptable while still being wrong for the consumer. Meaning validation is where those domain contradictions are flagged before a Tonic creates runtime objects.")>
  )>

  <section @{type:string = "feature", id:string = "tonics"} (
    <kicker("Tonics")>
    <title("Materialization happens after representation and meaning are accepted.")>
    <copy("A Tonic is a deterministic materializer that consumes AES, optionally relies on AEOS and meaning-validation guarantees, and creates a chosen output: a runtime object, graph, UI tree, exported file, minimized form, domain class, or rendered document.")>
    <code(`
accepted:AES
  → Greeting tonic
  → runtime Greeting object

accepted:AES
  → Template tonic
  → rendered HTML

accepted:AES
  → Audit tonic
  → signature report
`)>
    <copy("Transformation belongs at the edge of the system, not at the center.")>
  )>

  <section @{type:string = "feature"} (
    <kicker("Channels")>
    <title("The ecosystem keeps channels distinct.")>
    <referenceList(
      <item(
        <name("AEON")>
        <definition("human-readable declaration language.")>
      )>
      <item(
        <name("AES")>
        <definition("canonical assignment event stream and tool handoff surface.")>
      )>
      <item(
        <name("AEOS")>
        <definition("schema and form validation.")>
      )>
      <item(
        <name("Meaning Validation")>
        <definition("consumer-owned context checks over otherwise sound AEON claims.")>
      )>
      <item(
        <name("Canonical Form")>
        <definition("stable representation for comparison, signing, hashing, and reproducible processing.")>
      )>
      <item(
        <name("Annotation Stream")>
        <definition("structured semantic commentary and consumer-defined side-channel languages.")>
      )>
      <item(
        <name("&ND")>
        <definition("native documentation language in the broader AEON ecosystem.")>
      )>
      <item(
        <name("NEON")>
        <definition("text/binary hybrid transport in the broader AEON ecosystem.")>
      )>
    )>
  )>

  <section @{type:string = "feature", id:string = "contracts"} (
    <kicker("Contracts")>
    <title("Schemas, profiles, and conventions answer different questions.")>
    <copy("The ecosystem has multiple contract types because not every promise is the same kind of promise.")>
    <referenceList(
      <item(
        <name("Schemas")>
        <definition("form validation contracts: is this structurally acceptable?")>
      )>
      <item(
        <name("Profiles")>
        <definition("interpretation contracts: how should this be interpreted if the consumer trusts the profile?")>
      )>
      <item(
        <name("Conventions")>
        <definition("shared ecosystem contracts: what assumptions do compatible tools agree to use?")>
      )>
    )>
    <copy("A document can declare a schema, profile, or convention. The consumer still decides whether that declaration maps to something trusted.")>
    <code(`
meeting@{
  target:string = "zone"
  zone:string = "Australia/Melbourne"
  tzdbVersion:string = "2026b"
}:datetime = 2026-06-06T10:10:00
`)>
    <copy("For example, the temporal convention treats these attributes as a shared interpretation agreement. AEON Core keeps them as ordinary metadata; a trusted temporal-aware consumer decides whether and how to apply them.")>
    <actions(
      <action @{href:string = "/aeon-language.php#temporal-convention", primary:boolean = true} ("See temporal example")>
    )>
  )>

  <section @{type:string = "feature"} (
    <kicker("Tolerance")>
    <title("AEON is human-friendly at the surface and hostile to semantic ambiguity.")>
    <copy("AEON can permit multiple deterministic surface representations when they produce the same unambiguous structure.")>
    <code(`
a:list<number> = [
  1
  2
  3
]

b:list<number> = [1, 2, 3]
`)>
    <copy("But that tolerance stops before semantics. AEON rejects hidden coercion, runtime guessing, parser-specific repair heuristics, and invisible interpretation.")>
  )>

  <section @{type:string = "copy"} (
    <kicker("Conclusion")>
    <title("AEON serializes declarations, not premature runtime meaning.")>
    <copy("Most formats serialize values. AEON serializes declarations. AES becomes the stable semantic ledger. AEOS constrains representations. Meaning validation applies consumer context. Tonics materialize accepted meaning. Materialization happens last.")>
    <copy("The system remains deterministic because each layer knows exactly what it is allowed to do, and what it must never do.")>
    <copy("AEON describes. AEOS validates form. Consumers validate meaning. Tonics materialize. Meaning is deferred until explicitly requested.")>
    <actions(
      <action @{href:string = "/walkthrough-processing.php", primary:boolean = true} ("Open processing walkthrough")>
      <action @{href:string = "/walkthrough-annotations.php"} ("Open annotations")>
    )>
  )>
)>
